WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.440
Instructor: Next up, I'd like to talk a little bit

00:01.440 --> 00:03.480
about the exercises in the course.

00:03.480 --> 00:06.660
A very important piece, easy to overlook as a student

00:06.660 --> 00:08.760
but I think are very important

00:08.760 --> 00:12.090
to really understanding and living and breathing Git.

00:12.090 --> 00:13.110
You have to practice it.

00:13.110 --> 00:14.640
It's not just the syntax.

00:14.640 --> 00:18.210
So throughout the course, there are exercises sprinkled in,

00:18.210 --> 00:19.530
and I have to be honest,

00:19.530 --> 00:22.590
creating these exercises is not always that easy because

00:22.590 --> 00:25.980
unlike other things that I teach, CSS or JavaScript

00:25.980 --> 00:28.410
or Python, where I can ask students to make something

00:28.410 --> 00:30.480
and very clearly set out, you know

00:30.480 --> 00:31.560
here are the expectations.

00:31.560 --> 00:32.850
Make this thing.

00:32.850 --> 00:35.100
With Git, it's not really about making things,

00:35.100 --> 00:36.870
it's about the workflows.

00:36.870 --> 00:38.340
So I had to get pretty creative

00:38.340 --> 00:40.500
and I tried my best to come up with exercises.

00:40.500 --> 00:42.300
I'm pretty happy with how they turned out.

00:42.300 --> 00:44.040
I just wanna show you where to find them

00:44.040 --> 00:46.050
and the basics of how they work.

00:46.050 --> 00:49.590
So the exercises are at the end of sections.

00:49.590 --> 00:52.650
So for example, this is a section intro to GitHub.

00:52.650 --> 00:55.860
The very last video is GitHub Basics exercise

00:55.860 --> 00:58.740
or here's undoing changes in history.

00:58.740 --> 01:01.950
The last video is undoing changes exercise.

01:01.950 --> 01:05.160
So there are videos where I explain the exercise.

01:05.160 --> 01:06.600
I may mute this here

01:06.600 --> 01:09.570
so I explain how it works and what I'm expecting you to do

01:09.570 --> 01:13.320
and then I go ahead and do it myself, sort of a solution.

01:13.320 --> 01:15.510
But I always recommend you try it yourself first.

01:15.510 --> 01:18.720
And then if you want the actual exercise document,

01:18.720 --> 01:21.420
for every single one of these in resources,

01:21.420 --> 01:23.730
you'll find a link, click it, and it will take you

01:23.730 --> 01:25.950
to the exercise document that I created.

01:25.950 --> 01:28.680
So it has the instructions, the different files.

01:28.680 --> 01:30.240
There might be some starter code

01:30.240 --> 01:33.660
that I give you, the different expectations step-by-step

01:33.660 --> 01:35.040
what I'm asking you to do.

01:35.040 --> 01:36.780
So one of these documents exists

01:36.780 --> 01:38.550
for every exercise in the course

01:38.550 --> 01:42.660
and you'll find it attached to the exercise video.

01:42.660 --> 01:44.310
So that's where you can find them.

01:44.310 --> 01:46.800
Again, I hope that you get something out of them.

01:46.800 --> 01:49.020
The only way you'll know is if you actually attempt them.

01:49.020 --> 01:51.750
So I really encourage you to do this in person.

01:51.750 --> 01:53.910
When I teach Git the most learning

01:53.910 --> 01:55.800
without a doubt happens when students actually

01:55.800 --> 01:57.900
try using it and just get practice.

01:57.900 --> 02:00.180
It's a weird thing to watch somebody use Git.

02:00.180 --> 02:03.540
You really need to do it yourself to get anything out of it.

02:03.540 --> 02:05.730
Anyway, I hope you enjoy the exercises.

02:05.730 --> 02:06.780
That's how you find them.

02:06.780 --> 02:07.613
Please do them.
